WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.980
Our last classes followed voltages and currents continuously.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.580
Digital logic groups signals into two states instead: zero and one.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.580
Under positive logic, a low input represents zero and a high input represents

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:14.960
one.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.020
Real circuits use specified voltage ranges for those states.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.920
One is not universally exactly five volts, and an intermediate voltage is not

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.760
automatically a valid third logic state.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.540
For this lesson we use ideal stable binary inputs and ignore propagation

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:29.940
delays.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.340
A logic gate maps input states to an output state according to a rule.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.900
Start with the simplest rule: NOT.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.240
It has one input and reverses it.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.100
Input zero gives output one; input one gives output zero.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.420
The output is often written as A with a bar above it, read as not A.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.280
In a gate symbol, a little circle, or bubble, indicates inversion.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.940
It is a notation mark, not an extra external component that must be drawn as

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:56.920
another separate gate.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.600
Picture an indicator that should turn on when a condition is absent.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.660
If A means the condition is present, NOT A means it is absent.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.420
Now pause: if the input changes from zero to one, what happens to the ideal

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:09.720
output?

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.940
It changes from one to zero.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.300
A truth table simply lists every input possibility and the corresponding

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:16.680
output.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.680
One input needs two rows.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.540
Two independent binary inputs need four rows.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.556
We will use that complete row list rather than trusting one convenient

00:01:25.556 --> 00:01:25.960
example.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.600
Let's add a second input.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.380
An AND gate produces one only when both inputs are one.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.552
Think of a device that may operate only when permission is present and a

00:01:38.552 --> 00:01:38.900
safety

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.020
condition is satisfied.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.440
If either condition is zero, the AND output is zero.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:50.600
In row order zero zero, zero one, one zero, one one, the outputs are

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.440
zero, zero, zero, one.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.120
An OR gate instead gives one when at least one input is one.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.946
Imagine an indicator that turns on if either of two independent sensors

00:02:01.946 --> 00:02:02.300
reports

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:02.860
a condition.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.240
Its outputs in the same row order are zero, one, one, one.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.280
Both inputs being one still gives one.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.480
This is inclusive OR, not the different exclusive OR operation.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.500
A useful notation trap: Boolean plus means OR.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.860
One plus one in this algebra is one, not the ordinary arithmetic result two.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.720
A dot means AND, so one dot zero is zero.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.900
You are combining truth states, not adding measured voltages.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:33.780
Pause for a comparison.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:35.900
Inputs are one and zero.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.120
What are the two outputs?

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.020
AND gives zero; OR gives one.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.960
Testing only the one one row cannot distinguish these two gates because both

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:45.740
give one there.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.000
A full truth table can.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.880
When a JEE question shows switches as an analogy, identify whether both

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.440
conditions are required or either condition suffices.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:00.385
The meaning of a gate comes from its rule, not from the everyday wording of

00:03:00.385 --> 00:03:00.560
the

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:01.880
word or in isolation.

00:03:02.328 --> 00:03:06.728
Now we build two very important gates by adding a NOT operation to the whole

00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:07.068
output.

00:03:08.028 --> 00:03:09.008
NAND means NOT AND.

00:03:09.748 --> 00:03:12.848
First evaluate the AND result, then complement it.

00:03:12.888 --> 00:03:18.838
The NAND outputs for zero zero, zero one, one zero, one one are one, one,

00:03:18.838 --> 00:03:19.888
one, zero.

00:03:19.888 --> 00:03:22.568
Its only zero occurs when both inputs are one.

00:03:23.628 --> 00:03:24.668
NOR means NOT OR.

00:03:25.248 --> 00:03:27.848
First evaluate OR, then complement the result.

00:03:27.848 --> 00:03:32.288
Its outputs in that same row order are one, zero, zero, zero.

00:03:32.848 --> 00:03:35.148
Its only one occurs when both inputs are zero.

00:03:35.688 --> 00:03:37.628
The placement of the bar or brackets matters.

00:03:38.128 --> 00:03:42.268
Not the whole expression A AND B is different from simply saying not A AND not

00:03:42.268 --> 00:03:42.528
B.

00:03:43.108 --> 00:03:48.808
For example, with A equal to one and B equal to zero, NAND is one, but not A

00:03:48.808 --> 00:03:49.948
AND not B is zero.

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:52.588
That is a quick counterexample to a common error.

00:03:53.148 --> 00:03:56.670
In a drawing, NAND looks like an AND gate with an inversion bubble at the

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:57.008
output.

00:03:57.008 --> 00:03:59.508
NOR looks like OR with that output bubble.

00:04:00.288 --> 00:04:04.108
Think for a moment: how can you distinguish NAND from NOR using just one input

00:04:04.108 --> 00:04:04.348
pair?

00:04:04.968 --> 00:04:05.708
Choose one zero.

00:04:06.408 --> 00:04:08.168
NAND gives one, while NOR gives zero.

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:12.048
The zero zero row would not distinguish them because both give one there.

00:04:12.048 --> 00:04:17.828
De Morgan's rules say NOT of A AND B equals NOT A OR NOT B, and NOT of A OR

00:04:17.828 --> 00:04:19.708
B equals NOT A AND NOT B.

00:04:20.288 --> 00:04:23.428
Notice that complementing the whole expression also changes the connecting

00:04:23.428 --> 00:04:23.908
operation.

00:04:24.192 --> 00:04:29.072
A JEE circuit often has more than one gate, but the method stays friendly.

00:04:29.872 --> 00:04:33.652
Work from inputs toward output, and label every intermediate signal.

00:04:34.672 --> 00:04:36.392
Consider A and B entering an OR gate.

00:04:36.892 --> 00:04:40.312
Its output X then enters an AND gate alongside NOT C.

00:04:41.132 --> 00:04:45.872
The final rule is Y equals A OR B, in brackets, AND NOT C.

00:04:46.332 --> 00:04:48.992
Let A be zero, B one, and C one.

00:04:49.312 --> 00:04:52.512
First, X is zero OR one, so X is one.

00:04:52.512 --> 00:04:54.732
Next, NOT C is zero.

00:04:55.672 --> 00:04:58.192
Finally, one AND zero gives Y equal to zero.

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:02.952
Do not skip the intermediate step and let the nearby one values persuade you

00:05:02.952 --> 00:05:04.792
that the final output must be one.

00:05:05.472 --> 00:05:08.552
Now keep A and B the same but change C to zero.

00:05:09.172 --> 00:05:12.612
NOT C becomes one, so the final AND output becomes one.

00:05:13.152 --> 00:05:17.432
This circuit requires at least one of A or B to be active while C is inactive.

00:05:17.932 --> 00:05:21.754
Its complete truth table has eight rows because three independent binary

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:22.072
inputs

00:05:22.072 --> 00:05:23.632
give two cubed combinations.

00:05:24.312 --> 00:05:27.852
A convenient order runs from zero zero zero through one one one.

00:05:28.312 --> 00:05:32.992
For this example, the output is one for zero one zero, one zero zero, and one

00:05:32.992 --> 00:05:35.152
one zero, and zero in the other rows.

00:05:35.852 --> 00:05:40.532
Take a short pause and verify the last one: one OR one is one, and NOT zero is

00:05:40.532 --> 00:05:40.872
one.

00:05:41.472 --> 00:05:45.812
Real gate outputs respond after finite delay; our stable input truth table

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:48.112
exercise ignores that transient behavior.

00:05:48.852 --> 00:05:53.152
For a worked exam question, translate each symbol, preserve brackets, and

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:54.152
compute one gate at a time.

00:05:54.456 --> 00:05:59.156
Let's finish with why NAND and NOR are called universal gates.

00:06:00.016 --> 00:06:05.476
Each type can be combined to realize NOT, AND, and OR, and therefore more

00:06:05.476 --> 00:06:06.656
general Boolean functions.

00:06:07.456 --> 00:06:08.136
Begin with NOT.

00:06:08.996 --> 00:06:10.476
Tie both NAND inputs to A.

00:06:10.976 --> 00:06:12.876
The output is NOT of A AND A.

00:06:13.396 --> 00:06:16.556
Since A AND A equals A, the result is NOT A.

00:06:17.336 --> 00:06:22.436
Tying both NOR inputs to A also gives NOT A, because A OR A equals A.

00:06:22.976 --> 00:06:26.116
These connections are allowed when the component supports the stated input

00:06:26.116 --> 00:06:29.936
wiring; do not confuse joining inputs with shorting two different outputs

00:06:29.936 --> 00:06:30.396
together.

00:06:31.236 --> 00:06:37.076
To make AND using NAND, calculate X as NAND of A and B, then invert X with a

00:06:37.076 --> 00:06:39.056
second NAND whose inputs are both X.

00:06:39.956 --> 00:06:41.636
Two inversions leave A AND B.

00:06:42.216 --> 00:06:47.516
For OR using NAND, first invert A and B with two tied input NAND gates, then

00:06:47.516 --> 00:06:48.876
NAND those inverted signals.

00:06:49.936 --> 00:06:51.596
De Morgan's rule gives A OR B.

00:06:52.296 --> 00:06:54.836
Try the pair A equal to zero, B equal to one.

00:06:55.356 --> 00:06:59.956
The input inversions are one and zero; NAND of one and zero is one, as OR

00:06:59.956 --> 00:07:00.476
requires.

00:07:00.736 --> 00:07:02.736
But do not stop after that one check.

00:07:03.576 --> 00:07:06.236
Compare all four rows before declaring two networks equivalent.

00:07:06.796 --> 00:07:08.756
A similar construction works with NOR gates.

00:07:09.476 --> 00:07:14.236
For JEE, recognize the inversion bubble, the five required gate tables, and

00:07:14.236 --> 00:07:18.356
simple networks without importing ordinary arithmetic into Boolean operations.

00:07:19.576 --> 00:07:22.336
Treat every internal wire as a named intermediate result.

00:07:23.016 --> 00:07:27.096
That turns a complicated looking drawing into a sequence of small, reliable

00:07:27.096 --> 00:07:27.576
decisions.
